course: religion-ethics
lesson: 74
title: Souls, ghosts and stories
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: «Do I have a soul? Where is it?» asks Ollie; many people believe each person has a soul, the part that thinks, loves and is really «you», but it cannot be seen or weighed like a heart or a brain.
explain: Christians believe God gives each person a soul that lives on with God after death; Hindus, Sikhs and Buddhists speak of rebirth; and some people believe that «you» are your brain and body, so philosophers have argued about souls for thousands of years.
explain: Ghosts are a different thing: there is no good evidence that ghosts are real, and the haunted houses of films and the headless horseman of a sleepover story are told to thrill and scare; Christians believe the dead are with God, not haunting houses.
explain: When Ella's cousin says her grandma's ghost visits at night, she is most likely dreaming of Grandma or feeling close to her, which is a normal part of missing someone and nothing to be afraid of.
explain: Stories and cartoons give us pictures, floating spirits, dead girls turning into angels, but in Christian teaching people do not become angels: angels are a different kind of being, and people stay themselves with God.
explain: The wise move with big questions is to ask them freely, listen to many answers, and be careful with jokes about death, because someone near you may be missing a person they loved.
fact: Many people believe each person has a soul, the part that thinks, loves and is really «them»; it cannot be seen like a heart or brain.
fact: Christians believe God gives each person a soul that lives on after death.
fact: Some people believe a person is their brain and body, with no separate soul; scientists study how the brain makes thoughts and feelings.
fact: There is no good evidence that ghosts are real (ghost videos and photos can easily be faked); ghost stories are told mostly to entertain or scare.
fact: Christians do not believe the dead come back as ghosts; they believe the dead are with God.
fact: People who miss someone often dream about them or feel close to them; this is a normal part of grief.
fact: The headless horseman is an old legend, made famous in a story by Washington Irving in 1820.
fact: People disagree about whether animals have souls; Hindus and Buddhists believe animals are part of the cycle of rebirth.
fact: Philosophers ask questions like «What is a person?», «What is a soul?» and «What makes me me?»
fact: Many Indigenous peoples, and the Shinto tradition of Japan, believe that nature has spirits; Christians believe God made nature and cares for it.
fact: In Christian teaching people do not become angels when they die; angels are a different kind of being.
fact: Jokes about someone's dead relative can hurt, even when nobody means harm.
exercise: Ollie asks if he has a soul. What do many people believe a soul is? | The part of you that thinks, loves and is «you» | A small organ just behind the heart | A shadow that follows you in sunlight | The air you breathe out when you sigh
exercise: Can a soul be seen on an X-ray? | No; it can't be seen like a heart or brain | Yes, as a small glowing spot in the middle of the chest | Yes, but only by special doctors | No, because it hides when doctors look
exercise: What do Christians believe about the soul? | God gives it, and it lives on with God after death | It is reborn as an animal straight away | It disappears completely at death | It stays in the house as a ghost
exercise: What do some non-religious people believe about «you»? | You are your brain and body | You are a ghost living in a body | You are a soul that came from a star | You are your name and nothing else
exercise: Holly asks if ghosts are real. What is the honest answer? | There's no good evidence that they are | Yes, scientists have caught many on camera | Yes, but only in very old houses | No, because they all moved away
exercise: Callum tells a story about a headless horseman. What is it? | An old legend told to thrill and scare | A true news report from last year | A Bible story about a soldier who lost his head in a battle | A warning about riding horses
exercise: Ella's cousin says her grandma's ghost visits her at night. What is most likely happening? | She dreams of Grandma or feels close to her | Grandma's ghost really comes back to her room every night to see her | Grandma is angry and wants revenge | The house is cursed and should be sold
exercise: What do Christians believe about the dead and ghosts? | The dead are with God, not haunting houses | All dead people become ghosts | Only bad people become ghosts | Ghosts are angels in disguise
exercise: Freddie asks if souls float out like the spirits in cartoons. What is true? | That's only a cartoon picture; nobody knows what a soul looks like | Yes, exactly like that, in a puff of smoke | Yes, but only when someone sneezes | No, souls fall down into the ground
exercise: Sophie's friend says animals don't have souls. What is true? | People disagree about it | Science has proved they don't | Every religion agrees they do | Only cats and dogs have them
exercise: Which faiths believe animals share in the cycle of rebirth? | Hinduism and Buddhism | Islam and Judaism | Humanism and atheism | Only Christianity, from the start
exercise: Josh dresses as a ghost and says «Boo, I'm your dead grandma!» His friend is upset. Why? | Jokes about someone's dead relative can hurt | His friend is just scared of all costumes, even the funny ones | Ghost costumes are against the law | His friend wanted to be a ghost too
exercise: What should Josh do next? | Say sorry and be careful with that joke | Say it again, only louder, so that it sounds funny | Tell his friend to stop being so soft about it | Blame his friend for having no sense of humour
exercise: Lucy's teacher asks what you'd ask a philosopher. Which is a philosopher's question? | «What makes me me?» | «What is 7 times 8?» | «What time is lunch?» | «Where is the nearest shop?»
exercise: Leo hears some people believe trees and rivers have spirits. Which tradition is an example? | Shinto in Japan | Methodism in England | The Catholic Church | Humanism
exercise: Mia reads that a dead girl becomes an angel. What does Christian teaching say? | People don't become angels; they stay themselves with God | Every good child becomes an angel with wings when they die, as a reward | Only girls become angels when they die | Angels are the ghosts of old people
test: «Ghosts are real, I saw one on a YouTube video, it's facts,» says a Gen Alpha boy in California (facts = true). What's the best reply? | Videos can be faked; there's no good evidence for ghosts | If it's on YouTube with lots of views, it must be true, so he's right | Ghosts only show up on camera | He should be scared to go home
test: «Me nan comes to me at night, honest, I seen her,» says Ella's cousin from Bristol. What's the kind and true reply? | You miss her so much you dream of her; that's normal | Nan's ghost is haunting you, so your family should move out of the house | You're making it up to get attention | Tell her to go away and never come back
test: «Headless horseman's real, he lives up the ginnel,» says a boy in Leeds (ginnel = alleyway). What's true? | It's an old legend, not a real person | He's real and lives in Yorkshire | He's real but only appears at Halloween | He's a saint from the Bible
test: What would happen if someone joked «Boo, I'm your dead grandma!» to a kid whose gran just died? | The kid could be really hurt | Everyone would find it equally funny | The kid would feel much better | Nothing; jokes never hurt anyone
test: The vicar says, very formally: «The soul, in Christian understanding, is not a vapour that escapes the body but the person as known and loved by God.» What does he mean? | The soul is the real «you» that God knows and loves | The soul is smoke that leaves your mouth | The soul only exists in church | The soul is another word for the brain
test: «Bro, my dog definitely has a soul, he's a total vibe,» says a teen in Toronto (a vibe = great to be around). Which is accurate? | People disagree; some faiths say yes, others no | Science has proved that dogs have souls, but cats don't | No religion says animals have souls | Every faith teaches dogs go to heaven
test: In a cartoon, a dead grandma becomes a glowing angel who grants wishes. What's the Christian view? | People don't become angels; that's a picture, not a teaching | That is exactly what the Bible says | Grandmas become angels, grandads don't | Angels grant three wishes to each family
test: «If you were a philosopher, what'd you even do all day?» asks Lucy's brother. Best answer? | Ask and argue about big questions like what a person is | Collect stones and label them | Count the stars every night and write down how many there are in a book | Write the rules of football
test: «Do trees have spirits? Is that a thing?» asks Leo in Melbourne. What's the fair answer? | Some cultures believe so; others don't; respect both | Yes; science has measured tree spirits with special instruments in forests | No; only stupid people believe that | Only Christians believe trees have spirits
test: «Lol Ella thinks her nan's a ghost, she's delulu,» says a girl at school (delulu = deluded). What's the kind response? | Ella misses her nan; dreams like that are normal | She's right, Ella is being silly and should grow up | Ella should be laughed at until she stops | Ella is lying to make friends
test: Myth to check: «When good people die, they become angels with wings.» | False in Christian teaching; angels are different beings | True; the Bible says so plainly, many times, about good people | True, but only for children | False; they become ghosts instead
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the evidence concerning the existence of ghosts? | No reliable evidence establishes that ghosts exist | Scientific investigation has conclusively demonstrated their existence | Ghosts have been observed by every person who has grieved | Their existence is affirmed in the Christian creeds
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bereaved child may believe herself to have been visited by a deceased relative. | Dreams and feelings of nearness commonly accompany grief | The deceased are compelled to return to their former homes | Such visits are the ordinary consequence of a funeral | The child has deliberately fabricated the occurrence
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Christian teaching on angels. | Angels are beings distinct from humans, and the departed do not become angels | All persons become angels upon their death | Only deceased children are transformed into angels | Angels are the ghosts of the departed
exam: Whereas a pupil makes a jest concerning the deceased grandmother of a classmate, ascertain the conduct most consistent with the matters taught. | An apology and greater care in respect of such jests | A repetition of the jest in a louder voice | A rebuke of the classmate for want of humour | A complaint to the head teacher by the pupil who jested
